  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry through mid week.\n\nConditions will remain tranquil as high pressure persists over \nthe Mid-Atlantic through Wednesday before gradually moving \noffshore. Large scale subsidence and dry air lead to a mostly\nprecipitation-free forecast with temperatures rising to several \ndegrees above normal as the high pulls offshore.\n\nA weak surface trough in the wake of gradually departing high\npressure may be enough to produce a few showers mainly over the\nShenandoah Valley/I-81 corridor into the eastern WV panhandle\nfrom late Wednesday morning through late Wednesday afternoon.\n\nHigh temperatures may approach 90 Wednesday (slightly cooler in\nthe mountains). Low temperatures (and dew points) will be in \nthe 50s and 60s during this time. Some river valley fog is \nlikely each morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return Thursday.\n\nThe upper-level ridge will begin to flatten out Wednesday before \nbreaking down and pushing south and east into Thursday. Several \npieces of upper-level energy rotating around an upper low \ncentered over Hudson Bay will slowly push a cold front south \ntowards our region by Thursday. At the surface, high pressure \npushing offshore will bring increasing warmth and moisture \nto the area, with highs in the 80s to near 90 especially south\nof US-50/I-66 with dew points in the upper 60s to low 70s. Mid-\nlevel moisture will be increasing on westerly flow, but that\nintroduces a downsloping component in the lower levels. This\nreduces the amount of CAPE/DCAPE, especially when given\nanticipated cloud cover over the northern half of the area, for\nany potential severe weather. Still, a few clusters particularly\nfrom central VA to southern MD could contain gusty winds and\nheavy downpours Thursday, mainly in the afternoon and evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Cooler and less humid conditions are expected \non Friday, before humidity builds back in this weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will build to our north on Friday in the wake of \nThursday's frontal passage. Cooler and drier air will advect into \nthe region from the north in advance of the approaching high.\nPartly to mostly sunny skies are expected, with high temperatures\nin the upper 70s and lower 80s. It will also feel considerably \nless humid, with dewpoints dropping into the upper 50s and lower\n60s. Some shower or thunderstorm activity is possible near and\nsouth of I-64 as the front stalls just off to the south.\n\nHigh pressure will move offshore and a strong area of low pressure \nwill track across Hudson Bay on Saturday, causing winds to turn out \nof the south in response. Southerly winds will advect a more humid \nairmass back into the area, with dewpoints climbing into the upper \n60s and lower 70s. Chances for showers and thunderstorms will \naccompany the more humid airmass. Both instability and shear values \nare forecast to remain relatively modest, so any storms that do\nform likely stay on the weaker side.\n\nThere is still some model divergence for Sunday. What we do know is \nthat the humidity will stick around, bringing chances for showers \nand thunderstorms. However, there is still some uncertainty as to \nwhether or not the marine layer will mix out. If not, temperatures \nwill be cooler with plenty of clouds. If so, there will be some \nbreaks of sunshine and it will be warmer. Guidance leans slightly \ntoward the second scenario, but this will have to be monitored as it \ngets closer.\n\nHigh pressure may return behind a weak cold front for Monday and \nTuesday, bringing drier conditions, but temperatures will most \nlikely still be above climo.",
